I'm sorry, but I don't have the capability to directly modify or access your project files or tools to hide the project name in mobile mode. However, I can certainly guide you on how to do it!

Here's a general approach you can take:

1. **Identify the element**: First, you need to identify how the project name is being displayed in your HTML. It might be in a `<div>`, `<span>`, `<h1>`, or another HTML element with a specific class or ID.

2. **Use CSS to hide it on mobile**: You can use media queries to hide this element on mobile devices. Here's an example:

```css /absolute/path/to/style.css
@media (max-width: 768px) {
  .project-name {
    display: none;
  }
}
```

In this example, `.project-name` is the class of the element displaying the project name. The `@media (max-width: 768px)` rule applies the `display: none;` style to this element when the viewport width is 768px or less (a common breakpoint for tablets and mobile devices).

3. **If the project name is in JavaScript**: If the project name is being inserted or manipulated with JavaScript, you might need to add a condition to hide it on mobile devices. Here's an example:

```javascript /absolute/path/to/script.js
if (window.innerWidth <= 768) {
  document.querySelector('.project-name').style.display = 'none';
}
```
